Ukraine’s Main Intelligence Directorate (GUR) “Prymary” Special Unit carried out precision strikes on several high-value Russian assets in the Crimean Peninsula, including:
• Baltiсa-B coastal surveillance radar;
• Neva-B radar station;
• Pole-21 electronic warfare system;
• Strela-10 air defense system;
• Command post of the 503rd Motor Rifle Regiment;
• 4 communications antennas;
• 2 relay stations for Geran/Gerbera UAVs;
• Cargo truck;
• Ural truck;
• Patrol boat;
• Project 05060 landing assault boat;
• Mariya vehicle ferry;
• Kasta-2E2 radar.

Ukraine’s Unmanned Systems Forces has released footage showing strikes on 14 Russian aviation and air-defense targets, including a carrier-based Su-33 Multirole Fighter, a MiG-29 Multirole Fighter, an Orion UAV, and several radar and air defense systems.
11 of the 14 targets were successfully engaged in Russia’s Rostov Oblast and Krasnodar Krai, while the remaining three were in Crimea.
List of targets:
• Su-33 Multirole Fighter, Vityazevo, Krasnodar Krai, Russia;
• MiG-29 Multirole Fighter, Vityazevo, Krasnodar Krai, Russia;
• Orion UAV, Vityazevo, Krasnodar Krai, Russia;
• Tor air-defense system, Pudovyi, Rostov Oblast, Russia;
• Nebo-U radar, Dolotinka, Rostov Oblast, Russia;
• Nebo-U radar, Sevastopol, Crimea;
• Kasta 2E2 radar, Latonovo, Rostov Oblast, Russia;
• 48Ya6-K1 radar, Pryhibskyi, Krasnodar Krai, Russia;
• 36D6M radar, Uyutne, Crimea;
• Kasta 2E2 radar, Shabelskoye, Krasnodar Krai, Russia;
• Pantsir-S1 air defense gun/missile system, Yeysk, Krasnodar Krai, Russia;
• S-400 SAM system position, Gelendzhik, Krasnodar Krai, Russia;
• ST-68 radar, Yevpatoria, Crimea;
• 48Ya6-K1 radar, Golovatovka, Rostov Oblast, Russia.
